## 1. Introduction
Early cancer diagnosis is key to decrease mortality rates and improve survival rates [1,2]. Thus, developing credible, sensitive, rapid, and effective approaches for detecting cancer biomarkers or living cancer cells is of great importance. The traditional techniques, including X-ray, computed tomography (CT), transrectal ultrasonography, positron emission tomography (PET), ultrasound, magnetic resonance imaging, PET/CT etc., mainly focus on the morphological analysis of cells or tissues. Though remarkable progress toward diagnosing cancers at early stage has been achieved by such techniques, most of them lack satisfactory sensitivity, accuracy, and specificity for clinical diagnosis, resulting in misdiagnosis or missed opportunities for treating cancer patients at the best possible time [3,4]. Thereby, great efforts have been exerted to develop new techniques involving immunology, biochemistry, and molecular biology. These approaches can improve the sensitivity of cancer diagnosis by identifying abnormal bioactive species related to or released from the cancer organs or cells (e.g., $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}S,$ , NO, $0_{2}$ , and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ ) [5], abnormal expression of tumor biomarkers [2,4], or living cancer cells [6] in the biological samples. The common methods for early cancer diagnosis include surface plasmon resonance (SPR) [7,8], gel electrophoresis [9], surfaceenhanced Raman spectroscopy [10,11], colorimetric assay [12,13], fluorescence methods [14,15], electrochemical (EC) assay [16,17], electroluminescence [18,19], immunohistochemical assays [20,21], and radio-immunoassay [22,23]. EC sensing strategies are considered as ideal tools for cancer detection for their intrinsic merits, such as cost effectiveness, simple instrumentation, small sample volume requirement, fast response, high sensitivity, portability, reusability, and easy disposability [4,24,25]. Biosensors are compact analytical devices and considered promising clinical early-diagnostic techniques. In vitro biosensors are specific analytical tools for monitoring and prognosis using blood, urine or tumor tissues containing cancer markers, small molecules, enzymes, proteins, and living cancer cells [26–29]. Three integrated components are needed to design efficient EC biosensing systems: (i) biorecognition elements, such as aptamers, antibodies, or enzymes, that can interact specifically with the targets [30–33], (ii) signal transducers that can generate measurable signals due to the interactions between biorecognition elements and analytes, and (iii) electronic systems for data management [2,34,35]. The tight and specific interactions between biorecognition molecules and analytes can produce detectable EC responses on the biosensor surface. Changes in the electrical signals of the expected EC reactions, including electron transfer resistance, surface conductivity, and redox reaction potential, can then be monitored on electrode surface [36,37]. The signal transduction of EC biosensors, which is critical to improve their sensitivity and selectivity for target analytes, is largely dependent on electrode modification [38]. To enhance the sensing performances, such as sensitivity, selectivity, and response speed, and ease of fabrication of biosensors, several researchers have exploited carbon-based nanomaterials [39,40], conductive polymers [41], quantum dots (QDs) [42], noble metals [43,44], metallic oxides [40,45], and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) [46]. These materials modify the electrode surface as signal transducers and present the advantages of high signal amplification, easy preparation, multi-functionality, and remarkable thermal stability. In this context, various MOFs have been prepared and used as platforms of biorecognition elements for application of EC biosensing, including the early diagnosis of cancers [1,47–49]. MOFs are porous crystalline materials constructed from metal nodes (metal clusters or ions) and organic ligands via coordination bonding, which exhibit valuable intrinsic features, such as structural diversity, large surface area, high porosity, adjustable pore size, remarkable absorbability, and diverse functionality [4,38,50,51]. Thus, MOFs have been extensively applied in a variety of domains, such as gas storage [52], contaminant sorption [53], separation [54], catalysis [55,56], drug delivery [57,58], cancer therapy [59,60], and chemo/biosensing [61,62]. The study on MOFs-based sensors has rapidly expanded in recent decades (Fig. 1). The first work on MOFs-based sensor was reported in 2003 by Maspoch et al. [63], and MOFs-based EC biosensors for cancer or tumor diagnosis have been gradually explored since 2014.
MOFs are outstanding candidates for constructing EC sensors because they offer favorable environments for anchoring biorecognition molecules [38]. Compared with EC biosensors based on conventional materials, such as graphene oxides [64,65], transition metal oxides [66,67], or conductive polymers [68,69], those based on MOFs often show ultrahigh sensitivity or ultralow limit of detection (LOD; e.g., picogram per milliliter level) for detecting cancer biomarkers. Thereby, fabricating MOFs-based EC biosensors for detecting cancer biomarkers, the level of which is normally very low in tumor issues, is a worthwhile endeavor. The high sorption capacity of MOFs for diverse probe molecules is mainly due to two aspects. (i) Most MOFs are tailored by the functional groups (such as $\boldsymbol{-}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ or –COOH) and probes could be immobilized over MOFs-based substrates via covalent bonds [46,70,71]. (ii) Other types of binding forces, such as hydrogen bonding, $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking, and electrostatic forces, between the MOF linkers and biorecognition probes can also lead to the high adsorption of probes on MOFs [72,73]. These properties endow MOFs with superior sensing performance compared with other materials, such as metal nanoparticles (MNPs) [74], metal oxide nanoparticles (NPs) [75] and QDs [76]. A variety of MOFs-based biosensors have been developed for detecting heavy metal ions [77], antibiotics [78,79], cancer markers [1], RNAs/DNAs or microRNAs (miRNAs) [80] and living cells [48]. Some MOFs show the enzyme-like properties for amplifying EC signals [48,81], and those also showing fluorescence properties endow them with both EC and luminescent properties that could enable the construction of electrochemiluminescence (ECL) biosensors for cancer diagnosis [82–84]. Several reviews on biosensors based on MOFs [85–95] and their composites [38,96,97] have been published. For instance, Cruz–Navarro et al. reviewed MOF composites with excellent electrocatalytic activity toward glucose and secondary metabolites [85]. Pang et al. discussed the structure–property relationship and strategy for improving the sensing performance of MOFs-based EC sensors [93]. Chuang et al. also developed strategies to overcome the inferior chemical stability of MOFs in aqueous solution as well as their low electrical conductivity [86]. Moreover, miRNA and living cancer determination has become increasingly popular [91]. However, the review on cancer detection with MOFs-based EC biosensors is not available. As such, a comprehensive review on MOFs-based EC biosensors for early cancer determination, considering the practicability and development prospects of these materials, is necessary.
This review details the recent progress on EC biosensors based on MOFs and their nanocomposites for detecting diverse targets (e.g. cancer markers, microRNA, and living cancer cells) that are considered as indicators for the early diagnosis of cancers (Fig. 2) [98,111–115]. The nanostructures or nanocomponents of MOFsbased materials, and the working mechanisms of biosensors are discussed. The challenges in developing EC biosensors for early cancer diagnosis are also analyzed. The present review will provide deep understanding and new insight on the design and application of MOFs-based materials in electrochemical biosensing.
## 2. Working mechanisms of electrochemical biosensors
## 2.1. Working mechanisms for detecting diverse analytes
Along with the development of synthetic technology for MOFs and MOFs-based composites, the study on MOFs-based EC sensors for detecting cancer biomarkers and living cancer cells has been explored. The general construction process of MOFs-based biosensors for analyzing cancer markers includes three main steps (Fig. 3): (i) modification of the electrode surface with MOF materials, which usually exhibit EC activity, signal amplification ability, and high bioaffinity to different probes, (ii) immobilization of probes on the modified electrode, and (iii) identification of target analytes and production of EC signals.
First, ideal electrode materials based on MOFs should meet the following requirements: nanoscale size, good dispersibility and stability in aqueous solution, biocompatibility, high EC activity, and strong anchoring ability toward probe molecules. Only a few types of MOFs have been developed as the platforms for EC biosensors because of their poor EC activity [116–119]. Thus, great effort has been devoted to improve the EC activity or amplify the EC response of MOFs to boost the sensing ability of biosensors. Besides the utilization of conducting or semiconducting MOFs [49,73,120] (Fig. 4a), three methods have been applied to enhance the EC activities of MOFs-based materials: (i) integration of MOFs with other electrochemically active components (e.g., MNPs, carbon materials, and conductive polymers) [121–125] (Fig. 4b), (ii) preparation of bimetallic MOFs [6,47,101] (Fig. 4c), and (iii) annealing of MOFs to form nanocomposites with porous carbon or inorganic nanomaterials [106,126,127] (Fig. 4d).
Second, different probe molecules, such as aptamers, antibodies, and DNA strands, can be anchored on MOFs-modified electrodes by chemical bonding or physical interactions [6,129,130]. Given the unique merits of MOFs-based materials, a large amount of probe molecules could be stably adsorbed on MOFs-based platforms [71]. Thus, the working mechanism of MOFs-based EC biosensors is different to those of fluorescence sensors [131] or other EC sensors fabricated by multiple steps [132]. For example, the aptamers immobilized on MOFs-based materials usually cannot be released after binding with the targets by forming Gquadruplexes [101,126]. Furthermore, treatment with blockers, such as bovine serum albumin (BSA), glycine, and 6-mercapto-1- hexanol, is often used for EC biosensors to avoid non-specific adsorption between targets and carriers [106,133,134]. However, in the case of MOFs-based biosensors, blocking is unnecessary when the MOFs can cover the electrode surface, because the anchoring sites of MOFs would be fully occupied by the probes [135]. Some biosensors based on MOFs or MOFs-based composites have been developed without the blocking step, thus greatly simplifying the fabrication process, shortening their detection time, and improving their efficiency [79,101,136,137]. Three types of MOFs-based EC biosensors for determining analytes related to cancer have been developed (Fig. 5). MOFs-based nanomaterials could be directly used as platforms for adsorbing probes (e.g., aptamers, antibodies, or other probes); the determination signals of these materials depend on the changes in EC performance at the interface between modified electrode and electrolyte. MOFs-based materials carrying matching probes can be used to form hairpinlike probes with other probes on the sensor surface, which is accompanied by changes in EC signals at interface. MOFs or MOFs-based composites can be used as catalysts to achieve changes in EC signals and detect electrochemically active components in water solution. As illustrated in Fig. 3, four main types of detection mechanisms are applied to develop MOFs-based EC biosensors, including (i) specific binding ability of antibody with antigen, (ii) formation of G-quadruplet structure between aptamer and its target, (iii) hybridization of RNA or DNA chains, and (iv) living cancer cell analysis with abnormally expressed biomarkers. MOFs-based sensing strategies by these approaches have been applied for the sensitive and selective detection of diverse biomarkers in the early determination of various cancers (Fig. 2).
## 2.2. Principle of electrochemical techniques
Various EC techniques using functionalized electrodes, including EC impedance spectroscopy (EIS), square wave voltammetry (SWV), cyclic voltammetry (CV), linear sweep voltammetry (LSV), amperometric method, chronoamperometry (CA), and differential pulse voltammograms/polarography (DPV/DPP) have been applied in the field of bio-detection. EC measurements can also be combined with luminescence or a photo-based technique, such as ECL, photoelectrochemistry (PEC), electrochemical SPR, and EC– quartz crystal microbalance methods. Each technique presents unique characteristics, electrode configurations, applied signals, measured signals, mass transfer mechanisms, combination information provided [138], and target size selectivity [139]. EC methods for cancer diagnosis can be separated into potentiometric, amperometric, conductometric, impedimetric, and ion-charge/ field-effect approaches, based on the measured signal [138]. A detailed description of the available EC techniques has been published elsewhere [140]. The working principles, characteristics, requirements, and performances of some most commonly used EC techniques are briefly described below.
As a controlled-current method, potentiometry involves the measurement of electrical potentials under an applied lowamplitude current, which can be directly used to detect biomarkers or cells [140,141]. To our knowledge, potentiometric sensing system based on MOFs has not been reported. For conductometric method, the biosensors can easily be simplified using a twoelectrode configuration, and their differential measurement mode results in less susceptibility to interferences [142–144]. Therefore, conductometric techniques have been used for detection of cancer biomarkers due to their low detection limit and fast responses [145]. In addition, chronoamperometric methods provide the measured potential with the change of current, which are also used to monitor the cancer biomarkers [144,146,147].
CV is a common linear-scanning potential-controlled EC technique that is widely used for detecting cancer biomarkers [144,148]. CV is often taken in EC study because it provides a qualitative analysis of EC reactions [149]. More importantly, CV provides an efficient mean to measure and analyze redox potentials, test EC reactions, and probe electron transfer kinetics [150]. Hence, working together with EIS, CV is often utilized to probe the redox mechanism of biological molecules and qualitatively determine the EC activity of materials [6,148]. Compared with the traditional voltammetry technique, pulse voltammetry has improved speed and sensitivity due to the low influence of capacitive charging on current [151,152]. Anodic stripping voltammetry (ASV) technique has been mostly used to determine trace amounts of metal ions, and can also be used for early detection of biomarkers by coating metallic rich materials on electrode surface for current measurement [153].
For example, we previously applied CV to investigate EC conductivity during the fabrication and detection procedures [154]. Hong et al. [155] used SWV with $\mathsf{P b}$ -MOF functionalized dendritic DNA probes for determining carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA). Wang et al. [156] used SWV with an off–on signal amplification strategy for cytokeratin antigen 21-1 (CYFRA21-1) detection with a LOD of $0.65~{\mathrm{pg}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{mL}}^{-1}$ $(\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{N}=3)$ . Hatami et al. [157] applied DPV with an EC aptasensor based on MOF-reduced graphene oxide nanocomposite for detection of MUC1 with LOD of $0.033\mathrm{\pM}$ $(7.5~\mathrm{pg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}.$ ). Guo et al. [46] used DPV and EIS to detect CEA, with a lower LOD of $4.93\mathrm{\pg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ for the former. ASV has also been used to analyze cancer biomarkers, with a low LOD at the picogram per milliliter level [153].
Moreover, EIS techniques demonstrate the advantages of wide detection range and fast response [158]. Impedance-based EC methods generate biosensor responses based on the variation in impedance or calculated equivalent circuit elements [81]. For examples, we used EIS to detect mucin 1 (MUC1), CEA, living cancer cells, and CA 125 [6,49,73,154] with MOFs-based biosensors. Li et al. [98] used EIS for miR-122 determination by a biosensor based on MOF microcrystals. Ma et al. [159] used EIS for NSE determination based on $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MnO}_{2}}$ UNs/Au@Pd^Pt NCs with a low LOD of 4.17 fg $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ .
Moreover, ECL shows enhanced selectivity, simplicity, and reproducibility, which endow it with ability to analyze multiple components in real samples using multi-electrodes. Due to the regeneration of ECL, emitter occurs after ECL launches. Thus, ECL is usually a nondestructive system [95] and an effective analytical technology [84,160–162]. Any analyte that causes variation in ECL emission can be precisely analyzed [163,164]. Biological recognition elements and ECL-active species are critical in a typical ECL sensing system. Some luminophores, such as Ru complexes [110], luminol family members [165], and QDs [166], have been applied as ECL-active species [163]. Unlike ECL, photoinduced chemical phenomena related to charge separation or transfer and a semiconductor electrode are needed in PEC systems [167].
As aforementioned, various EC techniques involve different testing mechanisms and characteristics. Thus, two or more EC techniques can be integrated to promote the reliability of testing results. For example, Shao et al. [114] combined CV, EIS, and ECL for miRNA-141 detection based on Ru-MOF-derived ECL biosensors. EIS and DPV were used for signal detection of a Cr-MOF embedded with Co phthalocyanine-based sensing system for colorectal cancer (CT26) cell analysis [168]. Further, CV and DPV were combined by Ou et al. for detection of breast cancer cell [109].
## 2.3. Optimizing strategy of MOFs for electrochemical biosensors
As aforementioned, MOFs show great potential applications as EC biosensors [93]. However, MOFs often suffer from their chemical instability, insufficient conductivity, and large size of particles, which hinder their extensive development as EC biosensors. To solve these problems, the performance of MOFs could be improved by the following methods: (i) Selecting suitable ligand and metalbased nodes to fabricate MOFs. Some carboxylate-based linkers and metal ions in high valence state could offer the chemical stability according to the hard/soft acid/base principle. The common MOFs used in EC biosensor are constructed by the carboxylatebased linkers and imidazole-based linkers. [49,128,159] (ii) Introducing other metal ions to prepare bimetallic MOFs or MOFs-based hybrids doped with metal ions, which could establish effective electron transfer route by offering more active sites. (iii) Preparing the conductive 2D MOFs, 2D MOFs possess nanometer thickness and large surface area to volume ratio, thus providing platforms to anchor more probe molecules and electroactive species. (iv) Fabricating MOF derivates by calcination, which can inherit the high porosity and large surface area from their MOF parents. Meanwhile, the MOFs-based derivates containing metal nanoparticles, carbon materials and metal oxides can improve the conductivity and stability. (v) Constructing MOF hybrids by coupling with other MOFs, polymers or covalent organic frameworks (COFs), which combine the advantages of MOFs and functionality such as electrical conductivity and biocompatibility of other materials. Therefore, MOFs-based materials can serve as excellent platforms for detection of cancer biomarkers by establishing a well-designed system. In the following discussion, we will review the MOFs-based EC biosensors for detection of cancer biomarkers in detail.
## 3. MOFs-based electrochemical biosensors for detecting cancer biomarkers
Increased levels of tumor markers in body fluid excretions and cancer tissues produced by abnormal biochemical properties and metabolism of cancer cells are associated with the growth of certain tumors [169,170]. Tumor markers, such as telomerase activity, adenosine triphosphate (ATP), CYFRA21-1, protein tyrosine kinase7 (PTK7), CEA, lymphocyte activation gene-3 (LAG-3) protein, MUC1, nuclear matrix protein-22 (NMP-22), human epidermal growth factor receptor-2 (HER2), $\textsf{\textsf{a}}$ -fetoprotein (AFP), prostatespecific antigen (PSA), carbohydrate antigen 19-9 (CA19-9), carcinoma antigen 125 (CA125), carbohydrate antigen 15-3 (CA15-3),
CA 242, neuron-specific enolase (NSE), and various miRNAs (e.g., miRNA-122, miRNA-155, miRNA-144, and miRNA-21), are related to different types of cancers and, thus, can be used as indicators for diagnosing various cancers, identifying benign and malignant tumors, screening high-risk populations, judging the development various tumors, observing and evaluating tumor therapies, predicting the recurrence and prognosis of tumors, determining suitable treatment plans, and evaluating new drugs [2,43,169,171,172]. The sensitive, selective, credible, and rapid analyte assays for measuring these biomarkers are valuable tools for diagnosing cancers. Some biomarkers and their related cancers, as well as the determination techniques for these markers, are listed in Table 1. Common sensing techniques for biomarkers include SPR, colorimetry, CL, PEC, ECL, fluorescence, immunoradiometric assay (IRMA), enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA), mass spectrometry, electrochemistry, and surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy. EC techniques afford an effective and available approach for detecting cancer markers. EC biosensors are ideal assays for accurate and sensitive detection, and the development of diverse nanomaterials to modify electrodes plays a crucial role in improving the sensitivity and selectivity of the corresponding biosensors.
## 3.1. MOFs for electrochemical biosensors construction
MOFs have recently been exploited for sensing diverse analytes, including oxygen [191,192], amines [193,194], aromatic compounds [195], explosives [196,197], DNAs [198], RNAs [199–201] and miRNAs [202]. Various MOFs-based materials, including the monometallic MOFs, bimetallic MOFs, MOF@MOFs, MOFs-onMOFs, MOF@COFs, and MOFs-based nanocomposites, have been developed as electrode materials for the detection of cancer markers. However, only a limited number of monometallic MOFs, such as $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs (e.g., 509-MOF [73], 521-MOF [49], and UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ [176]), Ce-MOFs [203], porphyrinic MOF [204], and Pb-MOF [155], have been used as platforms for developing EC biosensors because most MOFs possess unsatisfactory electronic conductivity and reactivity [97,205–207]. MOFs with mixed metal centers have been prepared via the in-situ method, self-template method, selfassembly method, and one-pot method to improve their sensing performance and broaden their applications [208–210]. Bimetallic MOFs show enhanced framework hydrostability and excellent compatibility with different metal species [47,211]. The core–shell nanostructured MOF@MOFs or MOF@COFs demonstrate different levels of complexity with hierarchical structures. The layered structure of core–shell MOF@MOFs significantly affects the properties of materials [79,212]. Integrating different MOFs into one single hybrid system yields MOFs-based composites with enhanced properties, such as catalysis, sorption and electrochemistry [213]. Moreover, combining MOFs and other types of nanomaterials (NPs, nanofibers, biomolecules, or polymers) with high EC activity can highlight the merits of MOFs and nanomaterials [214,215]. Such approaches are effective to extend the applications of MOFs in sensing.
MOFs-based derivatives also show outstanding conductivity and superior biocompatibility. MOF-derived nanomaterials are often gained by thermal treatment of MOFs under different conditions. In such cases, MOFs are used as the sacrificial precursors or templates to form metal compounds, carbon materials, and their composites. Through an elaborate selection of MOFs with specific metal nodes and organic ligands as the parents, the MOFsderived metal oxides and their composites can integrate a variety of performance, thus improving electrochemical properties and biocompatibility. Meanwhile, MOFs-derived carbon materials and their metal oxide composites show more abundant applications in electrocatalysis, environmental remediation, and energy conversion [216]. Notably, annealing essentially changes the structures and chemical compositions of the original MOFs materials.
## 3.1.1. Monometallic MOFs
MOFs present notable advantages in the adsorption of antibodies [217], aptamers [218], and organic molecules [62]. Aromatic electron-rich ligands borne on MOF networks endow the latter with the ability to bind single-stranded DNA strongly via $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interactions, phosphopeptide anchors, and hydrogen bonds [201]. Such a characteristic is favorable for developing biosensors with greatly enhanced sensing performance toward diverse analytes. Some MOFs possessing remarkable EC properties or excellent electrocatalytic activities and showing feasible modification for electrodes can be directly applied to develop EC biosensors [49,176,203]. For instance, Zr-based MOFs normally display excellent stability and biocompatibility, as well as high affinity to the phosphate groups of oligonucleotide molecules or ribonucleic acid strands via the formation of Zr–O–P bonds. The good EC activity, high specific surface area, and strong bio-affinity of $\mathsf{Z r-O-P}$ between Zr-MOFs and aptamer strands render the former as promising platforms for EC biosensors [49]. Ce-MOFs and porphyritic MOFs exhibit good catalytic performance with amplified EC signals [203,204]. Electroactive $\mathsf{P b}^{2+}$ in Pb-MOF functionalized with dendritic DNA probes has been utilized as a signal tag to achieve multi-amplified signals [155]. Cu-MOF conjugated secondary antibody $(\mathsf{A b}_{2})$ combined with polydopamine was used to modify porous graphene immobilized by a primary antibody $(\mathsf{A b}_{1})$ ; the use of this sensor as an EC system for detecting CEA was subsequently explored, and a low detection limit of $0.025~\mathrm{\mathrm{ng}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}}$ was obtained [219].
Zr-MOFs can form $Z\mathrm{r}{-}\mathsf{O}{-}\mathrm{P}$ bonds between $Z\mathrm{{r}(\mathrm{{IV})}}$ clusters and phosphate groups on aptamers [120]. For example, a 509- MOF@Apt biosensor was developed via a one-step fabrication method through the formation of diverse functional groups; in this sensor, 509-MOF and a number of aptamer strands were integrated in the Zr-MOF network (Fig. 6a). This label-free EC aptasensor was used for the direct detection of various targets based on conformational changes in the aptamer strands when binding with the targets (Fig. 6b). The optimized 509-MOF@Apt-based aptasensor showed high stability, good repeatability, and acceptable applicability, and the work involved provided a new sensing strategy based on MOFs that could be directly and feasibly used to detect biomarkers [73,119]. 521-MOF nanosheets comprising octahedral $Z_{\Gamma_{6}}0_{8}(\mathrm{CF_{3}C O O})_{6}$ clusters as secondary building units was developed to construct an EC biosensor for the sensitive analysis of MUC1. The 521-MOF nanosheets showed good stability and EC activity, a large surface area, and strong bioaffinity to aptamers. Thus, large amounts of aptamers were strongly adsorbed onto 521-MOF through $Z\mathrm{r-O-P}$ bonds, $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking, and other interactions. The aptasensor was employed to analyze MUC1 via the EIS and SPR methods, and a low LOD of $0.12~\mathrm{pg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ in the concentration range of $0.001{-}0.5\mathrm{ng}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ was obtained (Fig. 6c) [49]. A multivariate $Z\mathrm{r}$ -porphyrin complex $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -TAPP) was prepared by the solvothermal method using mixed organic ligands and applied to fabricate a biosensor by adsorbing NSE antibodies to determine NSE [178].
Functionalizing MOFs can expand their applications in sensing field. An amino-functionalized Ce-MOF was prepared via onestep synthesis and applied for ATP detection by conjugation with the ATP aptamer [179]. The fabricated Ce-MOF-based biosensor showed selective and recyclable sensing properties and was further explored for analyzing ATP in human serum (Fig. 7a). A DNA-functionalized $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ was constructed to realize the ultrasensitive detection of CEA used the exonuclease-aided recycling amplification based on PEC biosensor. Herein, the UiO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ can release electron donors after the designed hairpin probe recognizing CEA, thus boosting the photocurrent to achieve a low detection limit of 0.36 fg $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ (Fig. 7b) [220]. ZIF-90 encapsulated with methylene blue was used to detect ATP by combining with fluorescence and DPV. Once the recognition of MB@ZIF-90 by target ATP, the MB@ZIF-90 decompose to bring up MB into the supernatant. Thus, the signals of fluorescence and DPV changed with the ATP concentration. This dual-signal strategy can be used to construct the label-free, enzyme-free and ultrasensitive biosensor (Fig. 7c) [221]. MOFs have also been used as carriers of bioenzyme and electroactive probe to fabricate biofuel cell-based self-powered biosensors. ZIF-8 encapsulated with glucose dehydrogenase was used as the anode to catalyze GDH. In addition, UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ enriched by electroactive species was used as a cathode to accept electron. Thus, a self-powered biosensor was obtained with dual MOFs as carriers. The biosensor showed excellent stability and sensitivity to exosomes, with the detection limit down to 300 particles $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ [222]. Using the functional ligands to synthesize MOFs is another efficient method to boost the sensitivity of biosensors and broaden the applications of MOF-related materials [223]. For example, because of the high porosity of porphyrinic MOF (PorMOF), each porphyrin unit can be accessed by substrates, and the dimerization of reaction centers can be prevented [180]. Thus, porphyrinic ligands have been widely explored for preparing MOFs that can serve as biomimetic catalytic platforms [224,225]. Ling et al. [223] designed a nanoscale PorMOF in which Fe porphyrin was used as the organic building block and Zr was used as the coordination centers; this material was subsequently developed into a biosensor to evaluate telomerase activity via CV and DPV (Fig. 7d). Once telomerase was triggered, a hairpin structure was formed between the assistant DNA 1 (aDNA1) and assistant DNA 2 (aDNA2) duplex, and aDNA2, which would be hybridized with capture DNA, was released. The electrode was further modified with a $\mathrm{PorMOF@SA}$ (streptavidin) tracer via the specific recognition between biotin and streptavidin, thereby generating strong EC signals. The LOD of the developed biosensor was 30 HeLa cells $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ within the concentration range of $1\times10^{2}–1\times10^{7}$ HeLa cells $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , and the telomerase activity within a single HeLa cell was evaluated to be $2.2\times10^{-11}$ IU within a single HeLa cell. This sensing system was employed to detect cell lines and verify the universal and reliable detection of telomerase via the proposed approach. The results indicate that PorMOF can serve as an efficient scaffold for transducing EC responses and may have wide application prospects for analyzing diverse cancer cells. Only few monometallic MOFs have been applied in this context because most of these materials have unsatisfactory EC activity, electron transfer capability, and biosecurity. However, monometallic MOFs have the simple composition and preparation process compared with other types of MOFs. Thus, exploiting new species of monometallic MOFs-based materials for analyzing biomarkers is necessary.
## 3.1.2. Bimetallic MOFs
Although combining metal NPs or carbon materials with MOFs can improve the EC activity of MOFs-based materials, their preparation procedures are fairly complex. The regular channels and nanostructures of MOFs are greatly disturbed by the addition of other components, which reduce their specific surface area, functionalization, or stability in water solution. These issues can remarkably affect the sensing ability of MOFs. An effective way to enhance the electron transfer and simplify the synthetic procedure of MOFs is to combine different metal components into one MOF or fabricate different MOFs. Bimetallic MOFs can be prepared using the one-pot method [101], step-by-step method [226], epitaxial growth approach [227], MOF-on-MOF method [47], template-directed approach [227], and in situ growth approach [228], among others. For instance, a conventional seed-induced growth procedure in which the pre-synthesized MOF cores act as seeds for growth of MOFs yielded core–shell-type products [226,229]. The one-pot method is generally used to construct modulated structures using mixed metal ions as the precursors [1,106]. A simple strategy for building core–shell MOF@MOF was recently explored [230]. The MOF-on-MOF method has also been explored for synthesis of bimetallic MOFs [6,47], especially in EC immunoassays. Bimetallic MOFs often exhibit improved sensing performances compared with their monometallic counterparts due to the synergistic and electronic effect of the included metals [106]. As a result, the bimetallic MOFs may provide diverse application prospects within a single product [6].
BA@AuNPs) [226]. The resulting MnFePBA@AuNP-based EC biosensor was used to detect HER2 with superior sensing performance (Fig. 8b), including low LOD, high selectivity, good reproducibility, and acceptable feasibility.
Two types of ZnZr-MOFs were synthesized via the MOF-on-MOF method to fabricate EC aptasensors for detecting PTK7 (Fig. 9a). The Zn-MOF-on-Zr-MOF composite exhibited a hierarchically decussated foliage and multilayered nanosheet structure. The corresponding $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF-on-Zr-MOF-based aptasensor displayed superior sensing ability compared with the Zr-MOF-on-Zn-MOF-based one, with low LODs of 0.84 and $0.66~\mathrm{pg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , respectively, within PTK7 concentrations ranging from $1.0~{\mathrm{pg}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{mL}}^{-1}$ to $1.0~\mathrm{\mathrm{ng}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}}$ via EIS and DPV measurements, respectively. Such excellent performance was mainly attributed to the presence of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF, which remarkably facilitates aptamer immobilization and stabilizes the formed G-quadruplex. The resulting biosensor provided a new approach for the applications of bimetallic MOFs in early cancer diagnosis [47]. Two types of TbFe-MOFs were utilized as platforms on which to anchor CA125-targeted aptamer for sensitive analysis of CA125 and MCF-7 cells (Fig. 9b) [6]. Though the two MOF-onMOFs showed similar structures, their surface nanostructures differed from their parent MOFs. As such, the constructed biosensors based on Tb-MOF-on-Fe-MOF demonstrated high stability of the Gquadruplex on account of the tight bioaffinity of the aptamer over Tb-MOF-on-Fe-MOF. The Fe-MOF-on-Tb-MOF-based biosensor supplied an extremely low LOD of $58~\upmu\mathrm{U}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ for CA 125; such a LOD is remarkably lower than those of other biosensors for detecting CA 125. Because of its good biocompatibility and enhanced endocytosis, the obtained Tb-MOF-on-Fe-MOF-based aptasensor showed high sensitivity for detecting MCF-7 cells [6].
## 3.1.3. Luminophore-combined MOFs
The ECL technique simultaneously highlights the advantages of the EC approach and the CL technique, including easy lightemission control, high signal-to-noise $(S/\mathsf{N})$ ratio, sensitivity and selectivity, and wide response range [103,166,231,232]. MOFsbased composites have shown potential applications in ECL biosensors. Ru-MOF composites illustrate excellent conductivity and inherent EC luminescence, thus showing promising sensing performance as ECL biosensors [103]. To date, however, no report on the direct application of Ru-MOFs for detecting biomarkers has yet been published. The stability of luminophore immobilization over MOFs originates from the high stability of the MOF structure, which can boost the sensitivity and stability of ECL luminophores [103]. Luminophores such as $\mathtt{R u}(\mathtt{b p y})_{3}^{2+}$ [103,182], QDs [97,233], Ag nanomaterials [234], and self-enhanced ECL materials [103] have been used to construct ECL analytical systems. As expected, MOFs combined with luminophores or some luminescent MOFs have been widely utilized in analyzing biomarkers via ECL.
$\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}^{2+}$ , which serves as an ECL luminophore that could be used to fabricate ECL sensors for the determination of biomarkers, generally provides stable and strong luminescent signal. Xiong et al. [103] prepared the self-enhanced Ru–polyethylenimine (PEI) complex-doped ZIF-8 (Ru-PEI@ZIF-8) to construct an ‘‘off– on” ECL biosensor for analyzing telomerase activity with high ECL efficiency (Fig. 10a). The prepared Ru-PEI@ZIF-8 nanocomposite acted as an effective ECL indicator that was absorbed on GO. Thus, energy transfer occurred between GO and Ru-PEI@ZIF-8. For this sensing approach, GO was used as ECL acceptor, while the Ru-PEI@ZIF-8 nanocomposite functioned as ECL donor. Because Ru-PEI@ZIF-8 is highly porous, the Ru-PEI complex within the external and internal layers of nanocomposite was excited by electrons, which remarkably enhanced the utilization ratio of the ECL materials. The developed ECL biosensor demonstrated good sensing ability for assessing telomerase activity in Hela cells and gave a LOD of $11~\mathrm{cells}.\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ within the detection range of $5\times10^{1}.$ – ${10}^{6}$ cells $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . This strategy was also used to detect telomerase activity in anticancer drug-treated cancer cells, and results indicated that the ECL biosensor has potential applications in anticancer drug screening. In $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)_{3}^{2+}{\cdot}U i O{-}66{\-}N H_{2}}$ , UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ acts as a carrier for the luminescent reagent $\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}^{2+}$ , which boosts EC luminescence signals; this material was used to construct an ingenious ECL immunosensor for CA15-3 (Fig. 10b) [177]. The aptasensor showed high sensitivity and selectivity for detecting CA15-3 with a LOD of $1.7705\times10^{-5}\mathrm{U}.\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ .
Compared to 3D MOFs, 2D MOF nanosheets show shorter transportation lengths for ions or electrons, thus improving the utilization ratio of luminophores [235]. Metal–organic layers (MOLs) can be feasibly modified with the luminophores, which possess higher loading ability toward luminophores than 3D MOFs [182]. Yao et al. [181] prepared a $\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{2}|$ (dcbpy)-doped 2DMOF nanoplate $\mathrm{Ru@}Z\mathrm{r}12$ -BPDC, BPDC $\mathbf{\Psi}=4,4^{\prime}$ -biphenyldicarboxylate, bpy $=2{,}2{'}$ -bi pyridine, $\mathrm{{H}}_{2}\mathrm{{dcbpy}}=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine- $^{.5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid), which showed remarkable ECL performance (Fig. 10c). The $\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{2}$ (dcbpy) bridged two $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Zr}}_{12}$ clusters of the $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Zr}_{12}$ -BPDC nanoplate, thus effectively inhibiting the release of Ru(bpy)2(dcbpy). Because the 2D $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Zr}_{12}$ -BPDC nanoplate exhibited high porosity and was ultrathin, it displayed superior EC activity for both the internal and external $\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{2}(\mathsf{d c b p y}).$ , thus further shortening the diffusion distance of different species, such as ions, electrons, coreactant (tripropylamine, TPrA), and co-reactant intermediates (TPrA and $\mathrm{TPr}\mathsf{A}^{\bullet^{+}}.$ ), and boosting the application of luminophores. MUC1 was modified into the H1-H2 duplex with the help of H1, H2, and a MUC1-catalyzed hairpin assembly (CHA). The constructed biosensor illustrated high stability and sensitivity, with a low LOD of 0.14 fg $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . Hu et al. [182] reported $\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{2}$ (mcpbpy)2+ loading capacity of Hf-MOL is 1.74 times higher of than that of a 3D mesoporous MOF (PCN-777), and the ECL efficiency and intensity of PEI@Ru-Hf-MOL were 1.27 times and 14.5 times higher than those of PEI@Ru-PCN-777, respectively. Accordingly, the PEI@Ru-Hf-MOL functioned as a good platform for constructing biosensors for detecting MUC1 (Fig. 10d).
Besides $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}^{2+}$ , some noble MNPs and metal QDs can also be used as ECL luminophores. For instance, Ag NPs were doped within Pb-MOF, the surface of which was modified with $\upbeta$ -cyclodextrin (Ag@Pb(II)- ${\bf\cdot\beta}$ -CD). The prepared Ag@Pb(II)- ${\bf\cdot\beta}$ -CD was subsequently exploited as a label-free ECL for analyzing PSA (Fig. 11a) [102]. Ag NPs enhanced the binding interaction between PSA antibody and $\mathsf{A g@P b(I I)-\beta}.$ -CD to improve the ECL intensity of Pb(II)- $\cdot\upbeta$ -CD. Similar to the ECL pathways of $\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}^{2+}$ , the ECL excitation route of $\mathsf{P b}$ (II)- $\cdot\upbeta$ -CD is oxidation-initiated reductive. Upon optimization, the ECL biosensor showed a LOD of $0.34~{\mathrm{pg}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{mL}}^{-1}$ $(S/{\mathsf{N}}=3)$ ) in the linear range of $0.001{-}50\mathrm{ng}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , as well as good stability, accuracy, and reproducibility. Fe-MOF was functionalized by N-(4-aminobutyl)- $N\mathrm{.}$ -(ethylisoluminol) and AuNPs (ABEI/MIL-101(Fe)) and thus, showed intrinsic mimic peroxidase activity to serve as effective ECL indicator to fabricate EC biosensors for MUC1 detection (Fig. 11b) [105]. For this sensing method, ABEI modified with 2- aminoterephhalic acid was regarded as an organic bridge ligand that could enhance the immobilization capability of ABEI. The ECL signal of ABEI/MIL-101(Fe) was substantially improved on account of its mimic peroxidase activity.
$\mathsf{A g^{+}@U I O{-}66{-}N H_{2}}$ was applied as a carrier for loading abundant of CdWS luminophores $(\mathsf{A g}^{+}@\mathsf{U I O-66-N H_{2}@C d W S})$ [84], and a novel ECL biosensor for PSA was developed (Fig. 11c). rGO and AuNPs were utilized to modify the glassy carbon electrode (GCE) surface and form a stable active area for anchoring the PSAtargeted antibody $(\mathsf{A b}_{1})$ , which was then bound to PSA via the interactions between antigen and antibody. ${\sf A g}^{+}@{\sf U I O-}66-$ $\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{C d W S}$ adsorbed with ${\mathsf{A b}}_{2}$ was regarded as a signal probe. After optimization of the experimental conditions, the ECL biosensor showed a low LOD of $0.038~\mathrm{pg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ within the concentration range of $0.1\ \mathrm{pg}.\mathrm{mL}^{-1}-10\ \mathrm{ng}.\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . CdSe QDs with high ECL activity were combined with MIL-101 to develop an ECL immunosensor for analyzing CEA [82] (Fig. 11d). This ECL immunosensor illustrated fast response to CEA, a LOD of $0.33~\mathrm{fg}.\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ within the linear range of $10^{-1\bar{2}}–10^{-4}\mathrm{\mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , and high sensitivity.
$2\mathsf{D}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{M}\mathsf{o}S_{2}$ QDs were integrated with a $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -based porphyrinic MOF (MOF-545-Zn) (Fig. 11e) [104] and then modified with alpha lipoic acid (LA). The obtained platform was explored as an effective ECL biosensor for analyzing CEA and gave a LOD of $0.45~{\mathrm{pg}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{mL}}^{-1}$ over a wide linear range of $0.18\mathrm{\ng}.\mathrm{mL}^{-1}-1\mathrm{\}\upmu\mathrm{g}.\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . An $\mathsf{M O F/A u/G-}$ quadruplex was developed by Shao et al. and employed as a simultaneous quencher/enhancer to construct a target-triggered ratiometric ECL sensor for PSA detection (Fig. 11f) [83]. With this strategy, the ratiometric ECL sensor developed with dualpotential-dependent ECL emitters (QDs and luminol) and $\mathsf{M O F}/$ $\mathsf{A u}/\mathsf{G}$ -quadruplex realized not only signal self-calibration but also comprehensive amplification. As expected, the sensor gave a detection limit of $0.058~\mathrm{{ng}{\cdot m L^{-1}}}$ toward PSA within the linear detection range of $0.5{-}500\ \mathrm{ng}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . Study on MOFs-based ECL biosensors is still in its infancy. Such biosensor systems may require the use of luminophores that play a crucial role for ECL biosensors. Future studies should focus on the development of high-performance and reliable MOFs-based ECL biosensors by improving the performance of luminophores.
## 3.2. MOFs composites for electrochemical biosensors construction
Although MOFs have been widely used in catalysis [236], sensing [237,238], and gas storage [239], their applications in EC biosensors are fairly limited on account of their low electronic conductivity and reactivity [97,205]. To overcome these shortcomings, porphyrinic MOFs have been designed and explored as EC biosensors [223]. Alternatively, combination with highly conductive and mechanically stable materials, such as noble-MNPs [124], carbon materials [240], conductive polymers [125], and so on [241], is vital (Table 2). Fortunately, MOFs have large surface areas and excellent absorption ability, thus can be used as nanocarriers for signal amplification following combination with EC-active components. Meanwhile, redox-active molecules or molecules with EC activity may also be applied to the porous frameworks of MOFs, to form a charge transfer complex with the metal clusters in the latter [189,242].
## 3.2.1. MOFs@metal NPs
Because conventional noble-metal NPs (NMNPs), such as Pt, Au, Ag, and Pd, exhibit remarkable electrocatalytic activity and excellent biocompatibility [129,243–245], they are often used to develop biosensors for small biomolecules (e.g., $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ NO, and dopamine) [246,247]. NMNPs can adsorb antibodies or immobilize the $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -labled aptamers, thus showing potential applications for fabricating EC biosensors [94,218]. However, free NMNPs are easily aggregated into large-sized particles, with resulting biosensors showing poor sensing performances [124]. Combining NMNPs with MOFs is an effective way to solve this problem. AuNPs were adsorbed onto GO-PB-PTC- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , which was achieved by integrating PB with GO and ${\mathsf{P T C}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ to enhance sensitivity of the resulting sensor for $\alpha-2,6$ -sialylated glycan detection (Fig. 12a) [183]. AuNPs and Cu-MOF nanocomposites $(\mathsf{A u}/\mathsf{C u M O F s})$ concurrently demonstrate EC and glucometer responses for the determination of DNA MTase [187]. Au-NP@Zn-MOF functionalized with a 1H-imidazolium-1,3-bis(2-aminoethyl)bromide ionic liquid was synthesized as photoactive element to prepare a PEC immunosensor for analyzing tumor markers. This immunosensor achieved a detection limit of $2.34~\mathrm{\pg}.\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ in the range of $5.0~\mathrm{pg}.\mathrm{mL}^{-1}{-}15.0~\mathrm{ng}.\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , thus showing potential use for detecting SCCA in clinical human serum samples [117].
Li et al. [128] built an EC biosensor on the basis of a chrysanthemum-like nanocomposite (Co-MOF/CuAuNWs), which was synthesized using Co-MOFs as carriers to wrap CuAu nanowires (NWs) for NMP-22 determination (Fig. 12b). A large amount of CuAuNWs was loaded onto the Co-MOF via the formation of Au– N bonds, resulting in strong catalytic activity. The designed biosensor showed remarkable sensing ability toward NMP-22, with a low LOD of 33 fg $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ $(\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{N}=3)$ over the concentration range from $0.1~\mathrm{pg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ to $1\ \mathrm{ng}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . Liu et al. [184] fabricated a voltammetric immunoassay biosensor with Ag-MOFs containing AuNPs (AuNP/ Ag-MOFs) as a platform to anchor the aptamer and detect CEA effectively via DPV. The as-obtained AuNP/Ag-MOFs not only adsorbed the CEA antibody but also amplified EC signals. Because the Ag-MOFs were composed of a number of $\mathsf{A g}(\mathrm{I})$ , they demonstrated the relatively stable EC signals. The proposed biosensor showed a low LOD of 8.0 fg $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ $(S/{\mathsf{N}}=3)$ over a wide linear range of $0.05{-}120~\mathrm{ng}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ .
Designing a hairpin DNA strategy for analyzing biomarkers based on noble metal-modified MOFs is another popular approach to design the EC biosensors. For instance, Dong et al. [185] developed a ratiometric EC biosensor for analyzing telomerase activity on the basis of AuNP-modified Ce-MOF (Au@CeMOF) combined with the conformational change of hairpin DNA. As shown in Fig. 12c, a hairpin probe with a telomerase primer (TP)-hybridized ‘‘stem” was modified by methylene blue (MB) and coated on electrode surface via Au–S bonds. In the presence of deoxynucleotides (dNTPs) and telomerase, MB was separated from the hairpin DNA. The TP-extended portion of the biosensor hybridized with the Au@CeMOF-cDNA tags, resulting in the variation of the EC response. The biosensor was used to explore telomerase activity in a single cell and revealed a LOD of 27 cells $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ in the concentration range of $2\times10^{2}–2\times10^{6}$ cells $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . AuNPs have been embedded within MOFs to act as an antibody carrier and construct biosensors for analyzing NSE. Au@MOFs with free amino group can substantially improve the adsorption of antibody. Embedded AuNPs can boost the EC activity of MOFs and accelerate interfacial electron transfer, thus improving the sensing performance of aptasensors [159]. The zeolitic imidazolate frameworks embedded with AuNPs (Au-ZIF-8) were applied to develop an EC biosensor for evaluating PKA activity [186]; this sensor gave a low LOD of $0.02~\mathrm{U}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ $(S/\mathsf{N}=3)$ ) over a wide linear range of $0.05{-}50\mathrm{\U}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$
Besides AuNPs, Au nanoflowers (Au NFs) have also been applied to improve electron transfer and provide a large EC surface area [99]. An Au NF-functionalized Cu-MOF nanohybrid was used to construct a ratiometric biosensor with hairpin DNA for detecting lipopolysaccharide (Fig. 12d); a good linear relationship in the range of 1.0 fg $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}{-}100\ \mathrm{ng}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ was obtained. Thus, this work supplied a new method for fabricating a redox MOF-based sensor with strong current responses. Bimetallic MOFs have been modified by AuNP metals to obtain novel materials such as MnFePBA@AuNP nanohybrids [226].
Zhao and colleagues [122] synthesized an AuNPs-PtNPs-MOFs nanomaterial with good biocompatibility and high conductivity and explored its use as a novel platform for immobilizing the antibody of NMP22; this biosensor was subsequently used to detect NMP22 (Fig. 13a) and showed sensitive responses toward NMP22 over a broad concentration range of $0.005{-}20\ \mathrm{ng}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ with a low LOD of $1.7\mathrm{pg}.\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\left(\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{N}=3\right)$ . Additionally, in order to improve the analytical performance of EC biosensors, the cascade reactions can be introduced by designing non-enzyme catalytic MOFs nanomaterials or MOFs encapsulated with enzyme. The cascade reactions are defined as a consecutive series of chemical reactions inspired by the bio-catalysis process involving domino, one-pot, or tandem reactions. Generally, enzyme or enzyme-like MOFs can convert analytes into indirect substrates with the increment of analytes, thus enhancing signal changes of the EC biosensors [248–250]. In such cases, enzyme or enzyme-like AuNPs can penetrate in the inner pores and surfaces of MOFs, benefiting for the enhancement of the signal output for EC biosensors. Therefore, the cascade reactions based on MOFs have received ever-increasing attention in the application of EC biosensors due to their high efficiency of chemical process and signal amplification. Zhang et al. [188] showed an amplification method for output signals by combining radical polymerization with impedimetric biosensing and then explored the capability of sensor for CA15-3 detection (Fig. 13b). As a type of peroxidase mimic, Cu-MOFintegrated CA15-3 ${\mathsf{A b}}_{2}$ and glucose oxidase $(\mathsf{G O}_{\mathrm{x}})$ were used as an immunoprobe to initiate radical polymerization and obtain poly(N-isopropylacrylamide) (PNIPAM) by cascade catalysis. PNIPAM, a resistance enhancer, was employed to cover the electrode surface and enlarge the resistance of the system due to its inferior conductivity, and AuNPs were coated on the electrode surface to amplify signals. The constructed biosensor showed wide detection ranges of $10\upmu\mathrm{U}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}{-}10\mathrm{mU}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ and $10\mathrm{\mU{\cdot}m L^{-1}}{-}100\mathrm{\U}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , along with an ultralow LOD of $5.06~{\upmu\mathrm{U}}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ for CA15-3 determination, revealing a new strategy for enhancing signals to analyze biomarkers.
Besides AuNPs, Au NFs, and AuNWs, AgNCs can also greatly accelerate the EC activity and excellent biocompatibility of MOFs. UiO-66 embedded with AgNCs prepared using the CEA-targeted aptamer as template (AgNC@Apt@UiO-66) was developed via a one-pot synthesis method and utilized to detect CEA (Fig. 12e) [46]. The EC aptasensor was obtained by simultaneous aptamer immobilization and MOF synthesis preparation and showed two advantages. First, the AgNCs were prepared using aptamer strands as a template, thereby avoiding the aggregation tendency of AgNCs; and second, UiO-66 embedded with the AgNCs showed enhanced EC activity, excellent biocompatibility, and high bioaffinity. Thus, the AgNC@Apt@UiO-66 composite showed the advantages of each of its components. The AgNC@Apt@UiO-66-based aptasensor exhibited superior sensing capacity for CEA via EIS. This approach could broaden the applications of MOF nanocomposites in biosensing and clinical diagnosis.
Electroactive amino-ZIF-67 loaded with PdNPs $(\mathsf{P d}/\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -ZIF-67) has been used as a new redox mediator to build EC immunosensor for analyzing PSA [100] (Fig. 13c). The PdNPs exhibited outstanding biocompatibility and superior catalytic performance, while the electroactive lamellar structures of ZIF-67 served as a carrier for PdNPs and a synergistic catalyst of Pd for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ decomposition. The immunosensor was then used to determine PSA via the amperometric method, with a low LOD of $0.03~\mathrm{pg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ in a wide detection range of 100 fg $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}{-}50\ \mathrm{ng}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . Wang et al. [124] fabricated an EC biosensor based on an amine-functionalized Cr-MOF loaded with PdNPs $\mathrm{(Pd/MIL101{-}N H_{2})}$ ) and used the obtained material to investigate telomerase activity (Fig. 13d); the resulting sensor displayed a low LOD of 11.25 HeLa cells $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ within a wide dynamic correlation of telomerase activity from $5\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~\times~}~}10^{2}$ to $1.62\times10^{7}$ HeLa cells $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ .
PtNPs have been integrated into MOFs to amplify EC signals and achieve good peroxidase-like catalytic activity. For example, a Pt@MOF-based biosensor (Fig. 13e) showed a wide dynamic correlation with telomerase activity within the range of $5\times10^{2}-$ $10^{7}$ HeLa cells $\mathbf{\cdotm}\mathbf{L}^{-1}$ by LSV and a telomerase activity of $2.0\times10^{-11}$ IU in a single HeLa cell [115]. A sensitive EC impedimetric aptasensor for CEA based on Cu-MOF loaded with PtNPs was developed [81] (Fig. 13f). Peroxidase-like Pt@CuMOFs with the G-quadruplex (hGq) were used for cascade catalysis amplification and greatly promoted the decomposition of the generated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Electron transfer in the sensing interface was effectively hindered, while EIS signals were efficiently amplified. The sandwichtype aptasensor based on $\mathsf{P t@C u}$ -MOFs showed a LOD of $0.023~\mathrm{pg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ for CEA detection within the concentration range of $0.05\mathrm{\pg}.\mathrm{mL}^{-1}.20\mathrm{\ng}.\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . Hence, noble metal nanomaterials have been employed to boost the EC activity and conductivity of pure MOFs, thus improving the EC performance of the corresponding biosensors for assessing biomarkers. Noble metal nanomaterials are favorable to improve the EC activity of MOFs on account of their many advantages, which include controllable particle size, superior EC activity, remarkable biocompatibility, and biosecurity. However, their preparation conditions are harsh and require high costs. Hence, non-noble metal nanomaterials with remarkable EC activity and biocompatibility are the candidate materials for modifying MOFs for biosensor construction.
## 3.2.2. MOFs@carbon materials
Owing to the good EC activity of carbon-based materials, such as graphene [251,252], carbon nanotubes [253], and CDs [254], they are widely used for fabricating EC sensors. Combining these carbon-based conductive materials with MOFs has revealed applications in EC biosensors [1,174]. MOF-rGO nanocomposite (CuMOF-RGO) was synthesized and used as a carrier to adsorb an MUC1-targeted aptamer and construct a new EC aptasensor for determining MUC1 via the DPV technique [157]. This sensor gave a low LOD of $0.033~\mathrm{pM}(7.5~\mathrm{pg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ for MUC1.
A number of aptamer strands can be immobilized on Zr-MOFs via $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{Z r}}-0-{\mathsf{P}}$ bonds. However, most $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -MOFs have poor EC activity [1], which remarkably limits their applications in EC sensors. CDs (size, $<10\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) demonstrate good hydrophobicity, high stability, superior electroactivity, excellent biocompatibility, low cost, feasible preparation, and high bioaffinity to aptamers [255]; thus with potential applications in aptasensors [256]. A bimetallic ZrHf-MOF embedded with CDs was synthesized to build a bifunctional biosensor for HER2 and MCF-7 and gave low LODs of 19 fg $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ and $23\mathrm{\cell{\cdot}m L^{-1}}$ , respectively [1]. However, although rGO, CDs, and $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ are popular materials used to modify MOFs, the complicated preparation process limits their application.
## 3.2.3. MOFs@polymers
Conductive and catalytically active polymers have been used to fabricate EC sensors based on MOFs for biomarker detection. For instance, thiolated polyaniline nanofibers were utilized to assist MOFs (Cd-MOFs/PANI or Pb-MOFs/PANI) and develop EC biosensors [153]. The biosensors showed detection limits of 0.03 and $0.1\ \mathrm{\pg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ for CEA and AFP, respectively, in the concentration range of $0.3~{\tt p g}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}{-3~\mathrm{ng}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}}$ . Polyaniline hydrogel and AuNPs were combined as a matrix to adsorb ZIF-hydroquinone (HQ)- bovine serum albumin (ZIF-8-HQ-BSA) for the development of an off–on immunosensor with amplified current density $(\Delta I)$ for analyzing CYFRA21-1 (Fig. 14a) [156]. Conductive polyaniline hydrogel and AuNPs can remarkably enhance the sensitivity of amperometric immunosensors by reducing the background signals. The ZIF-8- HQ-BSA probe initially displayed a weak current owing to the low electrical conductivity of ZIF-8 and BSA. Therefore, ZIF-8 was treated with HCl to enlarge the current ‘‘switch on” signal and release HQ, which was subsequently combined with the polyaniline hydrogel. HQ produced a substantial signal with outstanding catalytic ability for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to improve the response signal of the sensor further. The biosensor showed a low LOD of $0.65~{\mathrm{pg}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{mL}}^{-1}\left({\mathsf{S}}/{\mathsf{N}}=3\right)$ for CYFRA21-1 in human serum using SWV, and the developed amperometric immunosensing platform provided a new direction for clinical diagnostics, healthcare monitoring, and biomedical devices.
Li and coworkers [118] constructed an EC aptasensor with dual binding sites based on self-polymerized dopamine-decorated Au and Fe-MOF (Au@PDA@Fe-MOF), and then used as a biosensing platform for CEA detection. Since Fe-MOF contains abundant carboxyl groups and unsaturated $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ sites, $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -functionalized CEAspecific aptamer strands could be anchored to it via covalent bonds between amino and carboxyl groups. The aptasensor demonstrated a low detection limit of 0.33 fg $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ $(S/\mathbb{N}=3)$ for CEA detection in the range of 1 $\mathrm{fg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}-1~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . However, though conductive polymers can remarkably enhance the sensing ability of MOFsbased biosensors, their accessible preparation methods are quite limited.
## 3.2.4. MOFs-based derivatives
Owing that excellent electroactivity, porous nanostructures, and rich functionality, MOFs-based derivatives have shown great potential for detecting cancer biomarkers. In general, MOFsbased derivatives can be divided into three kinds of materials containing MOF-derived metal oxide and their composites, and metal decorated carbons materials. MOFs-based derivatives show outstanding sorption ability toward probes and enhanced electrochemical activity, thus improving the electrochemical response and boosting the accessibility of the aptamers, miRNA, and cancer biomarkers. In addition, their electron conduction further ameliorates the sensitivity, accuracy, and reliability of EC biosensors. According to the detection mechanism, EC sensors constructed by MOFs-based derivatives can be classified into electrocatalysisbased and aptamer-based EC sensors.
For example, Long et al. adopted the leaf-like Co-ZIF as the precursor to prepare hierarchical Co, N-CNTs electrocatalyst for detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The Co, N-doped hierarchical structure showed abundant active sites in favor of constructing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ nonenzymatic sensor. The resultant carbon cloth/ ${\mathsf{C o@C}}$ -CNTs nanohybrids showed a large electroactive surface area of $4.71~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and high sensitivity of $388~\upmu\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}\mathrm{M}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Moreover, this sensor possessed a low LOD of $0.27~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ with a linear range of $0.40~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}-7.2~\mathrm{mM}$ , by which in situ detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ released from MDA-MB-231 cells and HeLa cells could be realized [257]. To improve the electron conduction and catalytic properties of Co-MOF derivates, the Aunanoparticle-encapsulated ZIF-67 was directly used as template to prepare $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ dodecahedrons involved in carbon and Au nanoparticles $(\mathsf{A u@C-C o_{3}O_{4}})$ . The fabricated $\mathsf{A u@C-C o_{3}O_{4}}$ afforded excellent conductivity, analytic performance, and durability, which was attributed to its anisotropic architecture and the synergistic catalysis of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . The $\mathsf{A u@C-C o_{3}O_{4}}$ -based sensor presented remarkable electrocatalytic performance to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , giving a LOD of $19\mathrm{\nM}$ and sensitivity of $7553\ensuremath{\mathrm{\textrm{\textperthousand}}}$ . Especially, this assay was further used to monitor $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ released from HeLa cells and MCF-7 cells [258]. Apparently, it indicates that the Co-based MOFs are outstanding precursors offering abundant carbon and metallic oxides for development of electrocatalyst for the sensitive detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ released from living cells. $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ spinel and their hybrids derived from NiCo-based MOFs have been developed as platforms for detecting cancer biomarkers. Wang et al. used the CoNi-based ZIF nanosheets coupled with three-dimensional pRGO networks as the precursor for constructing the hierarchical $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}.$ - $\mathrm{O}_{4}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CoNiO}_{4}$ hybrids by pyrolysis at different temperatures. Moreover, a small number of metallic states of Co and Ni were embedded into $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}\mathsf{-C o N i O}_{4}$ hybrids, which improved the catalytic activity of hybrids. The 3D pRGO nanostructure provided a large specific surface area and fast electron transfer, offering a good platform to determine $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ released from living cancer cells. By this strategy, the nonenzymatic amperometric sensor was developed for analyzing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , showing a LOD of $0.41~\mathrm{{M}}$ in a range of
$5~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}{-}3~{\up m\mathrm{M}}$ and $3{\mathrm{-}}12{\mathrm{~mM}}$ . This assay was also applied to monitor $\mathrm{H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ released from living cancer cells, which exhibited good biocompatibility and applicability [259].
A similar method was used to finish the ultrasensitive detection of human immune deficiency virus-1 gene by replacing the 3DpRGO with carbon nanotubes. In addition, the ligand of NiCoMOF was changed to realize the formation of $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}0_{4}/\mathsf{C o O}_{I}$ CNTs by the pyrolysis in situ. Subsequently, the NiCo-MOF derivatives were applied as platforms for anchoring the HIV-1 probe DNA to detect HIV-1 target DNA by EIS, with a low LOD of 16.7 fM within the concentration of $0.1\ \mathsf{p M}$ to $20~\mathrm{{nM}}$ [253]. Similarly, the nanohybrids of nickel/ferric oxides and $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ were fabricated by pyrolyzing MOF-on-MOF of CoNi-ZIF nanosheet and CoFe PBA nanocube. The multicomponent nanohybrids possessed porous nanostructure and various metal oxides benefiting for the immobilization of aptamer strands, which was used for the detection of insulin via the formation of the G-quadruplex structure between insulin and aptamer. The LOD of the prepared EC biosensors was 0.16 fM within the range of 0.172 fM to $1.72~\mathrm{nM}$ [260]. Additionally, the bimetallic MOFs have been developed as a precursor for the synthesis of metal oxide and carbon matrix hybrids for the detection of cancer biomarkers. For instance, a series of cerium MOF nanohybrids have been prepared by doping with ferric or copper, and used as precursor for synthesis of bimetallic oxides and porous carbon. The EC sensor constructed by $\mathrm{CeO_{2}}/\mathrm{FeO_{x}}@\mathrm{mC}$ realized a sensitive detection of carbohydrate antigen 19–9 (CA 19–9) with an extremely low LOD of $10\ \upmu\mathrm{U}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . Besides, the CA 19–9 antibody was anchored to $\mathrm{CeO_{2}}/\mathrm{FeO_{x}}@\mathrm{mC},$ , which afforded the excellent specificity, good reproducibility, and stability of immunosensor [106]. A similar label-free aptasensor composed of cerium and copper oxides embedded within mesoporous carbon has been employed for the detection of tobramycin [78]. Hu et al. utilized the MnFe Prussian blue analog and polypyrrole as the precursor to synthesize the bimetallic MnFe oxides embedded within the carbon layer. This nanohybrid has been exploited as the scaffold to finish the sensitive detection of PTK7. The various functional N-related groups and synergistic effects among bimetallic oxide particles with amorphous carbon can improve the electrochemical activity and stability of the aptasensors. Therefore, the fabricated $\mathsf{M n O}_{2}/\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}@\mathsf{a C}.$ -based aptasensor showed a LOD of $44~\mathrm{fg}.\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ within the detection range of $0.1\ \mathrm{pg}.\mathrm{mL}^{-1}-10\ \mathrm{ng}.\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . Further, this aptasensor can be applied to detect human esophageal cancer EC9706 cells and exhibited a low LOD of 20 cells $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ within the range from 100 to $1\times10^{6}\mathrm{\cell{\cdot}m L^{-1}}$ [107]. In brief, MOFs-based derivatives own good electrochemical activity and porous structure benefiting for detection of cancer biomarkers.
## 3.2.5. Other MOFs composites
Besides carbon materials, polymers, and MNPs, MOF nanomaterials could also be modified with biomolecules or EC-active molecules. A type of dendritic DNA scaffold labeled with Pb-MOF (HCR-Pb-MOF) via the hybrid chain reaction has been applied as a signal tag for CEA [155]. Based on the one-step replacement of signal tags into the supernatant, a ‘‘one tumor marker-to-massive Pb-MOFs” amplification strategy was realized to ameliorate the detection sensitivity by SWV. An EC biosensor with amplified multi-signals and a zero background was developed and used to detect biomarkers rapidly. The biosensor showed a LOD of $0.333~\mathrm{pg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ for CEA over the concentration range of 0.0001–10 $0\mathrm{pg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ within 10 min. In addition, biofunctionalized MOFs were obtained by loading MB onto UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (MB@DNA/MOFs) and using programmably assembled DNAs as gatekeepers. The designed biosensor showed amplified signals and functioned as 2D trackers for biosensing. The cascade-amplified biosensor showed a LOD of $16~\mathrm{fg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ for CEA in concentration range of 50 fg $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}{-}10$ $\mathrm{\ng{\cdot}m L^{-1}}$ [189]. Tetracyanoquinodimethane (TCNQ)-doped Cu-MOF was synthesized to boost the electronic conductivity of the sensor. The composite acted as a novel platform for constructing the biosensor for PSA detection. The PSA antibodyconjugated TCNQ- $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}$ -based aptasensor achieved a LOD of $0.06~\mathrm{\mathrm{ng}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}}$ in the dynamic linear range of $0.1{-}100~\mathrm{\ng{\cdot}m L^{-1}}$ , as well as satisfactory specificity toward PSA in the presence of other proteins; thus, this sensor showed practical utility for spiked serum samples [207]. MB-glucose oxidase-ZIF-8/rGO-Au (MBGOx-ZIF-8/Au-rGO) was synthesized to develop an EC biosensor for CA 242 (Fig. 14b) [190]. The glucose-triggered biocatalytic and decomposition reactions of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -induced films were integrated at interface to enhance signals. The developed immunosensor had a wide linear range of $0.001{-}1000\mathrm{U}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ and an ultralow LOD of $69.34~{\upmu\mathrm{U}}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ $(S/\mathsf{N}=3)$ , thus providing a promising strategy for development of immunosensing platforms for other types of biomarkers.
The above approaches depend on signal amplifiers or indicators to resolve the poor EC activity of MOFs and construct complex sensing systems. However, these biosensors still suffer from the problems such as complicated manipulation and time-consuming fabrication procedures. An alternative approach to obtain suitable devices is to design and prepare conductive or semiconductive MOFs or EC-active MOFs.
## 4. MOFs-based electrochemical biosensors for detecting microRNAs
Different types of RNAs, such as mRNA, miRNA, rRNA, tRNA, siRNA, and long noncoding RNAs, are contained in cell transcriptomes. These RNAs can be regarded as crucial noninvasive or minimally invasive biomarkers because of their important effect on many pathophysiological phenomena and cellular processes [80]. The aberrant (i.e., up- or down-regulated) expression of miRNAs is usually related to diverse diseases [261]. Hence, miRNAs are considered biomarker candidates for cancer determination [262,263]. Advanced detection techniques that allow the ultrasensitive, simplified, and stable analytical analysis of miRNAs is especially vital for the early diagnosis of cancers [114]. MOFs-based materials that can be used to the development of EC biosensors are also emerging as an effective approach for analyzing miRNAs. For example, CuMOFs have sparked great interest in EC biosensors for detecting miRNAs on account of their well-defined configuration and remarkable catalytic activity [113,264,265]. The detection of miRNAs is generally based on the total hybridization of probe DNA strands with miRNA analytes. Single DNA strands can be strongly anchored over MOFs and MOFs-based composites via $\pi-\pi$ stacking, electrostatic, and hydrogen bonds [238,266]. MOFs not only strongly immobilize probe DNAs but also show resistance to nucleic acid-strand degradation from nucleases [71,267], which thus have been explored as the quenching labels for the detection of disease-specific nucleic acids. In these strategies, the metal clusters within MOFs can stimulate the intrinsic fluorescent quenching ability of the sensor via photoinduced electron transfer process once the organic ligands tightly bind the probe DNA [268]. Table 3 shows a summary of MOFs-based EC biosensors for miRNA determination.
A few sensors based on Ru-MOF have been reported for the detection of miRNA by using ECL. For instance, an ECL sensor for analyzing miRNAs was constructed on the basis of selectively and sensitively disassembled properties of Ru-MOF with ${\mathrm{Hg}}({\mathrm{II}})$ ions, producing strong ECL signals by releasing large quantities of $\mathsf{R u}(\mathrm{II})(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}$ ions [269]. The ECL sensor was developed by anchoring more hairpin probes H1 over Ru-MOFs, resulting in amplified signals. The designed ECL sensor had superior sensing properties and a low LOD of $0.3\mathrm{\fM}$ within the miRNA-155 concentration range of $_{0.8\mathrm{\fM}-1.0\mathrm{\nM}}$ . Jian et al. [269] reported an ECL biosensor for detecting miRNA-155 using Ru-MOF as the ECL signal amplifier (Fig. 15a). Shao et al. [114] constructed a Faraday-cage ECL biosensor for analyzing miRNA-141 using signal strand DNA (sDNA)-labeled Ru-MOF; here, the capture unit was synthesized by adsorbing DNA (cDNA) on the functionalized magnetic nanoFe $_3$ - $\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{S i O}_{2}@\mathsf{A u}$ nanospheres (Fig. 15b). Using the developed Faradaycage cascade, the ECL intensity increased with the logarithm of miRNA-141 concentration, and a low LOD of 0.3 fM over the concentration range of 1 fM–10 pM was obtained. Thus, the
Faraday-cage ECL sensing strategy shows promising prospects in the clinical detection of miRNA.
Additionally, MOFs have been modified by noble MNPs to analyze different miRNAs. For instance, Wang et al [113] presented an EC biosensor based on hairpin assembly target recycling to amplify signals and AuNPs-modified Cu-MOFs for the accurate analysis of miRNA (Fig. 16a). In this case, Cu-MOFs provided a large surface area for enveloping the AuNPs and anchoring the DNA strand 1 (S1) to enhance the detection signal. The sensor showed a LOD of 0.35 fM for detecting miRNA-155 within the wide detection range of 1.0 fM–10 nM. Given these characteristics, the developed biosensor showed good applicability for analyzing miRNA-155 from serum samples. Li et al. [98] prepared PdNP@Fe-MOFs to fabricate an EC biosensor that could quantitatively determine microRNA-122 (Fig. 16b). In this approach, streptavidin and biotinylated signal probes were used as tracer labels and conjugated with the PdNP@Fe-MOFs nanohybrid. The miRNA-122 target showed a sandwiched structure between the tracer labels and thiol-terminated capture probes inserted to the MCH monolayer, which was coated on N-doped graphene nanosheets functionalized with AuNPs $\mathsf{A u N P@N-G},$ . Because of the assembly of targetcatalyzed hairpins and the mimetic co-reaction of PdNP@FeMOFs for signal enhancement, the biosensor showed a LOD of 0.003 fM $(S/{\mathsf{N}}=3)$ within the concentration of range $0.01~\mathrm{fM}{-}10$ pM. The biosensor was subsequently used to determine miRNA122 in real biological samples and showed satisfactory results.
Most MOFs-based EC biosensors are fabricated for analyzing single targets rather than multiple analytes. Occasionally, however, one disease is simultaneously overexpressed with several biomarkers or miRNAs. Using one a single miRNA as a biomarker may lead to false positive results for clinical diagnosis due to insufficient information, anxiety for the affected individual, and unnecessary biopsy or surgery [270,271]. Thus, multiplex biomarker assays via EC measurement based on MOF platforms have been carried out to improve the degree of precision of cancer diagnosis [263]. Multiplex detection for analyzing coexistent biomarkers using a single biosensor is vital in real samples. For example, Chang et al. [112] reported an EC biosensor based on nucleic acid-modified MOF with label- and enzyme-free features for the simultaneous detection of let-7a and miRNA-21 (Fig. 17). Two functionalized MOFs (MB@UIO and TMB@UIO) loaded with electroactive dyes and covered with double-stranded DNA (dsDNAcapped MOFs) by in situ adsorption and hybridization of nucleic acid have been employed for the concurrent analysis of let-7a and miRNA-21 and showed LODs of 3.6 and $8.2~\mathrm{fM}$ , respectively.
Tian et al. [111] designed and developed a paper-based biosensor device based on MB and MOF for miRNA-141 and miRNA-21 detection (Fig. 18); the obtained platform featured a low impedance and large sensing area. MOF conjugated bio-probe, MB, and ferrocene (Fc) with distinguishable EC signals contributed to the good sensing performance of the aptasensor. The biosensor showed a LOD of 0.1 fM for the simultaneous detection of miRNA-141 and miRNA-21 under optimal conditions, thus showing potential applications in precise detection of targets in serum samples.
## 5. MOFs-based electrochemical biosensors for detecting living cancer cells
A large number of malignant growth cells can be released into tumor tissues, leading to cancer metastasis [272]. Given the rapid growth of cancer cells and fluctuation of biological state in disease condition, the detection of cancer cells can greatly maximize the potential of early therapeutic intervention and improve the survival rates [273]. The early diagnosis of cancer cells is helpful to therapy [272,274], and some techniques have been developed such as magnetic separation [275], flow cytometry [276], and fluorescence detection [277]. Unfortunately, though these methods have been used to capture and/or detect cancer cell, they also show several shortcomings, including high production cost, poor selectivity, low sensitivity, and complex detection procedures. EC analysis has been extensively developed to detect cancer cells because of their superior sensing performance and simplicity [1,226,278–280]. EC methods have recently been used for cancer cell identification, cell counting, and protein detection on cell surfaces [278]. Because cancer cells are overexpressed with glycans and proteins, they can be feasibly captured by the specific binding between glycans or proteins and probes (e.g., aptamer, lectin, and antibody). Aptamers show advantages, such as specificity, pH/temperature stability, easy chemical functionalization and immobilization, high efficiency, low cost, low immunogenicity, and cell-free chemical synthesis [126]. The information of immobilization amount and stability of probe carrier materials is essential for sensitive detection of cancer cells. EC biosensors based on MOFs or MOFcomposites for detecting cancer cells are listed in Table 4. These materials are not only good candidates for probe carriers but also demonstrate feasibility for developing EC biosensors.
For example, $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF with a mixture of AA and 2,5- diaminoterephthalic acid as ligands $(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}2\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ) was prepared for developing diverse aptasensors toward MCF-7 cells. The obtained aptasensor showed a LOD of 31 cell $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ with the detection range of 100–100,000 cell $\mathrm{\mL}^{-1}$ [154]. MIL-101 (Fe) with peroxidase-like activity and large surface area was used as a nanocarrier with improved catalytic ability. The corresponding EC cytosensor was used for HepG2 determination [48]. AuNPs were attached with MIL-101- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ via Au–N bonds to form MIL$101@\mathrm{AuNPs}$ , which were decorated by thiolated hybridization chain reaction (HCR) products to achieve enzymatic signal amplification. Aptasensors based on DNA nanostructure have been used to sensitively detect HepG2 via the multi-branched hybridization chain reaction amplification strategy (Fig. 19). The nanoprobes showed the features of high specificity and enzymatic signal enhancement. In the presence of nanoprobes, HepG2 cells were captured to form a DNA tetrahedron–cell–nanoprobe complex with sandwich-like structure. The obtained cytosensor showed a LOD of 5 cells $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ with the detection range of $10^{2}{-}10^{7}\mathrm{cells}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$
A dual-aptamer system has been developed to overcome the entanglement and aggregation of single-stranded DNA aptamers on electrodes, which remarkably hinders the binding of aptamers to cells. A sandwich-type cytosensor based on an MOF material (PCN-224) decorated with Pt NPs and DNA tetrahedron-linked dual-aptamer was constructed by Ou et al [109] to determine MCF-7 cancer cells (Fig. 20). The nanoprobes were employed for the EC oxidation of HQ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to enhance the EC signal. The biosensor showed good performance with a detection range of $20–1\times10^{7}$ cellsmL1 and a LOD of 6 cells $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . More importantly, the developed biosensor could release all components on the electrode surface via an EC reductive desorption to break gold-thiol bond and realize the regeneration of the cytosensor. This cytosensing strategy can also be extended to detect other types of cancer cells using specific aptamers, thus expanding its applications in point-of-care cancer diagnosis.
UiO-66 was functionalized by folic acid (FA) via coordinative interactions [116] and explored as a scaffold for an EC cytosensor for living cancer cell determination. FA was bound to folate receptors (FAR), which are overexpressed in different cancer cells. The FA@UiO-66-based biosensor revealed a low LOD of 90 cells $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ in the detection range of $1\times10^{2}–1\times10^{6}$ cells $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , and excellent selectivity for detecting FAR-rich cancer cells and FAR-negative cells. We also developed $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs [154], CD@ZrHf-MOF [1] (Fig. 8a), Fe-MOF-on-Tb-MOF [6] (Fig. 9b), and MnFePBA@AuNPs [226] for the detection of MCF-7 cells (Fig. 8b).
Determination of the cancer cells is the most direct approach for early diagnosis of cancer. The MOFs-based probe carrier materials for analyzing cancer cells must meet the following requirements: (i) The MOFs should possess excellent biocompatibility, further making cancer cells in vitro with strong activity; (ii) the size of MOFs-based nanocomposite should be smaller than $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ , leading to the cell endocytosis of these MOFs nanomaterials and improving the biosensor stability in aqueous solution; and (iii) it would be helpful that MOFs-based materials demonstrate strong fluorescence performance, to enable in vivo determination. Given these requirements, the determination of living cancer cells using MOFs-based EC biosensors clinically remains a distant goal.
## 6. Conclusion and future perspectives
EC biosensors based on MOF and MOF nanocomposites are promising analytical approaches for the early diagnosis of cancers owing to their high loading capability for the probe and resistance against probe degradation; these features can greatly enhance the detection selectivity of biosensors. MOFs-based EC biosensors with diverse sensing mechanisms can sensitively detect different targets, such as cancer markers, miRNAs, or cancer cells. Meanwhile, MOFs possess unique structural characteristics such as uniform pores, tunable compositions, high porosity, and large surface area, affording huge potential as EC biosensors to detect cancer biomarkers. The porous structures and large surface area offer an opportunity to anchor more enzymes, aptamers, electroactive species, and metal nanoparticles et al., which can greatly amplify the electrochemical signal and improve the limit of detection of targets. Furthermore, the effective mass transfer can be realized through the interconnected pore structures and surface of MOFs. The porosity also makes MOFs as substances to integrate with other functional materials for improving the sensing performances. In addition, the tunable chemical compositions of MOFs can be designed to interact with analytes or probes, thus enhancing the sensitivity and selectivity. Then, these unique structures also provide more active sites to react with other functional materials to construct MOFs-based hybrid materials for promoting sensing performances. Therefore, the unique structures of MOFs with excellent EC biosensing performances for cancer diagnosis. Nevertheless, the pristine MOFs face unprecedented challenges including poor electron conductivity, expensive ligand, large particle size, and instability, leading to their limit of application in EC biosensors. For example, monometallic MOFs seldom have been explored to construct biosensors for analyzing biomarkers or living cells due to their poor EC activity and monotonous features substantially limit their applications. Actually, these drawbacks can be improved by integrating noble metal nanomaterials, carbon-based conductive materials, and conductive polymers; designing 2D MOFs; and pyrolyzing MOFs into metallic oxides to improve electron transfer at the interface of platforms and electrolyte, and enhance the sensing performance of the resultant devices for analyzing living cells or biomarkers. Especially, 2D MOFs may be good candidates owing to their excellent conductivity and nanometer thickness. These MOF nanosheets show large surface areas, accessible active sites, and small diffusion barriers, which make them become an ideal matrix. Besides, the ligands of MOFs can be changed by different functional groups for introducing other materials such as COFs, MOFs, and enzymes. The MOFs-based hybrids possess the merits of both MOFs and guest materials, which offer more active sites, functional groups, conductivity, and specificity to overcome the disadvantages of pristine MOFs. In addition, combining CDs or other fluorescent materials with MOFs, except Ru-MOF, featuring excellent conductivity and intrinsic EC luminescence can endow MOFs with enhanced ECL signals and yield suitable ECL biosensors for detecting diverse analytes.
MOFs-based EC biosensors have also been constructed to detect dual- or multiple targets because single cancer targets may induce false positive results for clinical diagnosis based on insufficient information and the lack of specificity of aptamers immobilized over MOFs-based platforms. Bifunctional EC aptasensors for the detection of two or more targets based on MOFs or MOF composites have also been developed. Such an approach presents simple operating procedures, low detection cost, and specific adsorption. Hence, the development of multiple detection approaches without interference is a future research direction for MOFs-based EC detectors.
While many efforts have been devoted to achieve MOFs-based EC biosensing, some challenges remain. For example, although combining MOFs with other types of EC-active component is an efficient method to enhance the EC performance of MOFs-based sensors, the complex preparation procedures, high cost, and complicated structures of the composites limit their applications in biosensing and biomedical fields. The fabrication of heterostructures for integrating diverse types of MOFs may provide promising materials that can potentially cascade the various properties of different MOFs in some sequence to produce functions that cannot be achieved by single MOF layers. Thus, the use of heterostructures as platforms for EC biosensors may be an effective approach for MOFs-based biosensor fabrication.
The immobilization or adsorption mechanism of probes on MOF carriers is unclear. The immobilization behavior of aptamers or DNA/RNA strands on MOFs has not been completely studied, and no suitable or adequate research method to investigate whether the probe molecules are adsorbed on the MOF surface or penetrate the interior has yet been developed. The adsorption behaviors of DNA/RNA strands mainly depend on the intrinsic features and pore size of MOFs. However, further studies how aptamers or DNA/RNA strands change their conformations after binding with their corresponding targets must be conducted.
Multifunctional or bifunctional biosensors that can be used to detect multiple targets concurrently or separately are of great importance. The EC biosensors based on MOFs, MOFs-based composites, or heterostructures of diverse MOFs can not only detect cancer markers but also determine cancer cells. These MOFsbased nanomaterials clearly possess high EC activity, good fluorescence performance, small size, and good biocompatibility. These intrinsic properties endow the related materials with high sensing performance, good cell endocytosis, and remarkable cell imaging. Thus, the preparation of more MOF materials with which to construct advanced biosensors for tumor tissue detection is highly desirable.
At moment, the exploration of MOFs-based EC biosensors may be considered in its infancy. More improvements in terms of the ability of these sensors to diagnose cancer cells in whole blood must be implemented. Conductive MOFs can be applied to enhance the separation and transport efficiency of photogenerated electron/holes in self-powered Schottky diode devices for use as photodetectors. Such self-powered sensors have been exploited for harmful gases and vapors, as well as aptasensors for antibiotics. And the development of these materials as sensors with applications in cancer detection has yet to be successfully achieved. Wearable MOFs-based sensors have potential applications in nextgeneration artificial skins, personal healthcare monitoring, and human–machine interactions but not for cancer diagnosis.
Finally, the exploration of MOFs-based biosensors for cancer cell detection remains a challenging. No aptamer strand that can recognize specific cancer cells has yet been obtained. In addition, other requirements for MOF construction, such as small size $(<500\ \mathrm{nm})$ for feasible endocytosis, outstanding fluorescence performance, and excellent biocompatibility, must be satisfied. Thus, the development of MOFs-based EC cytosensors that can be used for the early diagnosis of cancer should be further studied.
